{"name":"Mica Creek","hex":"#70828f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mica","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of a group of hydrous aluminosilicate minerals characterized by highly perfect cleavage, so that they readily separate into very thin leaves, more or less elastic."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mica"},{"term":"creek","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small inlet, often saltwater, leading to the sea or to the main channel of a river, especially a river estuary.","The inner part of a port that is used as a dock for small boats."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/creek"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mica Creek","blocks":[{"text":"Mica Creek is a small village in British Columbia, Canada that was used as a base of operations for the construction of the Mica Dam hydroelectric project by BC Hydro in the 1960s and 1970s. It is located 148 km north of Revelstoke, British Columbia on Highway 23 and situated at the convergence of the Revelstoke Lake and a stream called Mica Creek. The village is effectively a relocation of Mica, which had been at the confluence of the Columbia and Mica Creek proper. The creek itself was named for the flakes of mica minerals which could be found floating in its waters. The Mica Creek area was one of the foci of the Big Bend Gold Rush of the 1860s."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Micapic.jpg/330px-Micapic.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mica_Creek","wikidata":"Q6827820"},{"title":"Mica","blocks":[{"text":"Micas are a group of silicate minerals whose outstanding physical characteristic is that individual mica crystals can easily be split into fragile elastic plates. This characteristic is described as perfect basal cleavage. Mica is common in igneous and metamorphic rock and is occasionally found as small flakes in sedimentary rock. It is particularly prominent in many granites, pegmatites, and schists, and \"books\" of mica several feet across have been found in some pegmatites."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Mica_%286911818878%29.jpg/330px-Mica_%286911818878%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mica","wikidata":"Q114675"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/MicaSheetUSGOV.jpg/500px-MicaSheetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/MicaSheetUSGOV.jpg/500px-MicaSheetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sheets of mica","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMicaSheetUSGOV.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Dark_Mica_from_Eastern_Ontario.jpg/500px-Dark_Mica_from_Eastern_Ontario.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Dark_Mica_from_Eastern_Ontario.jpg/500px-Dark_Mica_from_Eastern_Ontario.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dark mica from eastern Ontario","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADark_Mica_from_Eastern_Ontario.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Mica_T.png/120px-Mica_T.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Mica_T.png/250px-Mica_T.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Mica_T.png/120px-Mica_T.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMica_T.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb